Quothz wrote:

> I think the most important things to stress in the guidebook are:
> 
> (a) Your goal is to aquire the Amulet of Yendor and present it to
> your god, and
> (b) This is not going to be easy.
> 
> There's been some debate on the first point, and how specific it
> should be. In my (experienced!) opinion, figuring out what to do
> with the Amulet is much easier than figuring out, say, how to
> find and open Moloch's Sanctum.

You have experience in discovering how to do the invocation and what to 
do with the Amulet on Astral, both unspoiled?

There is at least an oracularity on how to do the invocation (although 
in the wrong order). Nothing of the sort, I believe, exists in-game on 
what to do with the Amulet on Astral. And the vibrating square is 
generally a much more peaceful place for figuring things out than the 
Astral plane is.

> I'm against tables of weapons, armor, and equipment, although
> it might be worth it to present a plain-language description of
> nonmagical starting items:
> 
> "Archeologists understand dungeons pretty well; this enables
> them to move quickly and sneak up on the local nasties.
> 
> "Archeologist enter the dungeon wearing an old leather jacket and
> a lucky fedora. He or she carries a bullwhip for driving away
> enemies (and perhaps a few stunt scenes), a pickaxe for digging out
> collapsed tunnels (and for dealing with more obstinate foes!), a
> sack for storing delicate finds, a tinning kit for preserving meat
> for later consumption, a touchstone for identification of valuables,
> and plenty of food. Some archaeologists are better-prepared than
> others, and yours might remember to bring a tin opener, a light
> source, or a writing tool."

No. Excess verbage to no purpose. You find out what each class has on 
the first turn of the game. Figuring out what purposes they serve is 
part of the charm of the game. ("Why would I need a blindfold?")

> I would even go so far as to remove the -current- listing of armor
> class by armor type.

Why? The relative value of types of armor should be apparent from its 
appearance, but all the player has is a name to go by. I'd include not 
only AC but weight (maybe on a relative scale, rather than absolute).

> Finally, a far more general suggestion. The guidebook is part of
> the game, and should be treated as such. In my opinion, the purpose
> of the guidebook should not be to make the game easier (I would
> delete all mention of Elbereth if it were up to me), but to make
> the game less confusing. The focus of the guidebook should be on
> helping the player interpret the game's output and on what action
> (but not result!) will take place for a given command.

Agreed in general; but how is the player supposed to figure out 
"Elbereth"? Yes, there's the inscriptions in Sokoban and the Castle, but 
  they have no effect on the PC, so why would you expect that they have 
any effect on anything else?

